/* Level 17: Deeper reading (about Grade 5 to 6). Reading between the lines, characters' choices and motives. */
S(17, "The Empty Chair", `For as long as Mehwish could remember, there had been an empty chair at the head of the dining mat. Her grandmother placed a cushion on it every evening, smoothed the embroidered cover, and set a glass of water beside it. Nobody ever sat there.

When Mehwish was little, she thought it was a game. When she was older, she assumed it was for guests. It wasn't until she was twelve that she finally asked.

Her mother hesitated, glancing at Grandmother, who was busy at the stove. "It was your grandfather's place," she said quietly.

"But Grandfather died before I was born."

"Yes."

Mehwish didn't understand. It had been fifteen years. Surely her grandmother didn't still expect him to come home. She felt a flicker of impatience, even irritation. The chair took up space. Her cousins had to squeeze together when they visited.

That winter, Grandmother fell ill. For two weeks she stayed in bed, and the chair stood bare. Without anyone saying so, the family stopped arranging it. One evening, Mehwish's uncle sat in it absent-mindedly while eating. Nobody objected.

But Mehwish noticed that her grandmother, when she was finally well enough to come to dinner, stopped in the doorway and looked at the chair for a long moment. Then she sat in her usual place without a word. She ate very little.

That night, Mehwish couldn't sleep. She thought about her grandmother placing the cushion every evening, year after year. She realised she had never once seen her grandmother cry about Grandfather. Perhaps the chair was where she put the crying instead.

The next evening, before anyone else arrived, Mehwish took the embroidered cover from the cupboard and smoothed it onto the chair. She placed the cushion exactly where her grandmother always placed it, filled a glass with water and set it down.

When Grandmother came in, she stopped again in the doorway. Her eyes moved from the chair to Mehwish, who was pretending to be very busy arranging plates.

Grandmother didn't say anything. But during dinner, she reached across and squeezed Mehwish's hand, and held it for longer than usual. After that, the chair was always ready. Mehwish made sure of it, even on the evenings her grandmother forgot.`, "embroidered, assumed, hesitated, irritation, absent-mindedly, objected, recognised, arranging",
"Whose chair was it?|Grandfather's, who died before Mehwish was born;Why do you think Grandmother kept setting up the chair?|It was her way of remembering and grieving for Grandfather;Why did Mehwish start setting up the chair herself?|She understood how much it meant to her grandmother and wanted to comfort her");

S(17, "The Scholarship", `When the letter arrived, Jahanzeb read it four times before he believed it. He had won a scholarship to a boarding school in Islamabad, one of the best in the country. Tuition, books, uniforms and meals would all be paid for. Only twenty students in the whole province had been chosen.

His teacher had encouraged him to apply. "You are capable of so much more than this village can offer you," she had said.

His mother wept with joy. His younger sisters danced around the courtyard. But his father, after reading the letter, said nothing at all. He folded it carefully, placed it on the shelf, and went out to the fields.

That evening, Jahanzeb found him sitting alone by the canal. "Abba," he said. "Aren't you happy?"

His father looked at the water for a long time. "Who will help me with the harvest?" he said at last. "Your brothers are too young. The land is too much for one man."

Jahanzeb's excitement drained away. He hadn't thought about that. He had been so busy imagining the school, the library, the science laboratories, that he had forgotten the family needed him here.

For three days, he barely spoke. He helped in the fields from dawn until dusk, trying to decide. Every option seemed to betray someone. If he went, he would be abandoning his family. If he stayed, he would be abandoning himself.

On the fourth evening, his father came to him with the letter in his hand. "I spoke to your uncle," he said. "His sons will help during the harvest. In return, I'll help with his wheat." He paused. "And you will come home every holiday and work twice as hard."

Jahanzeb stared at him. "But you said..."

"I said what a farmer says when he is frightened," his father interrupted. He looked away, toward the dark fields. "My father never let me finish school. I told myself it didn't matter. I was wrong." He pressed the letter into Jahanzeb's hands. "Go. Learn everything. Then come back and teach the rest of us what you know."

On the morning he left, his father walked him all the way to the bus stop, carrying his bag even though Jahanzeb could easily have carried it himself. Neither of them spoke much. But just before the bus pulled away, his father reached through the window and gripped his hand, hard.`, "scholarship, boarding, tuition, province, capable, betray, abandoning, interrupted",
"What did Jahanzeb win?|A scholarship to a boarding school in Islamabad;Why did his father seem unhappy at first?|He was worried about the harvest and frightened of losing his son's help;What does the father reveal about his own past?|His father never let him finish school, and he regretted it");

S(17, "The Rival", `Kamran and Daniyal had been rivals since the first year of primary school. If Kamran scored ninety-five in maths, Daniyal scored ninety-six. If Daniyal won the hundred-metre race, Kamran won the long jump. Their teachers called them the two lions. Their classmates placed bets on who would come first in each exam.

In their final year, the school announced that only one student would be sent to represent the district at the national science olympiad. The choice would be made based on a special test.

Both boys studied harder than they ever had. Kamran stayed up past midnight, working through old exam papers by the light of a small lamp. He knew Daniyal was doing the same.

Two weeks before the test, Daniyal didn't come to school. Then he missed another day, and another. Rumours spread. Someone said his father had lost his job. Someone else said he had gone to work in his uncle's shop.

Kamran felt a strange mixture of emotions. Part of him, a part he wasn't proud of, felt relieved. Without Daniyal, he would certainly win. But another part felt hollow. He realised that half of his motivation for studying had come from wanting to beat Daniyal.

On the fifth day, he walked to Daniyal's house after school. He found him in a small shop in the bazaar, selling plastic buckets and brooms, his schoolbooks stacked beside him under the counter.

"My father's sick," Daniyal said flatly, not meeting his eyes. "Someone has to keep the shop open. I'll come back after the exam. Maybe."

Kamran looked at the stack of books. He thought about all the nights he had studied, motivated by the thought of Daniyal studying too.

The next afternoon, he arrived at the shop with his own notes. "I'll mind the counter," he said. "You study. Then we'll swap."

Daniyal stared at him. "Why would you help me? You'll lose."

Kamran shrugged. "Maybe. But if I win without you there, it doesn't count."

For two weeks, they took turns at the counter and studied together at the back of the shop, quizzing each other on chemistry and physics.

Daniyal won the test by two marks. Kamran was disappointed for exactly one evening. The next morning, he was at the shop again, helping Daniyal prepare for the national olympiad.`, "rivals, represent, olympiad, rumours, relieved, hollow, motivation, quizzing",
"Why did Daniyal stop coming to school?|His father was sick and he had to keep the family shop open;What did Kamran realise about why he studied?|Half his motivation came from wanting to beat Daniyal;Why did Kamran say winning without Daniyal wouldn't count?|He wanted a fair contest, and beating someone who couldn't study wouldn't mean anything");

S(17, "Alexander Fleming and the Accidental Discovery", `In September 1928, a Scottish scientist named Alexander Fleming returned to his laboratory in London after a summer holiday. Fleming was brilliant but famously untidy. Before leaving, he had stacked glass dishes containing bacteria on a bench without cleaning them.

Sorting through the dishes, he noticed something unusual. One dish was contaminated with a fuzzy blue-green mould, the kind that grows on old bread. Around the mould, there was a clear ring where the bacteria had been destroyed. Something produced by the mould was killing the germs.

Many scientists would have simply thrown the spoiled dish away. But Fleming was curious. He grew more of the mould and tested it. He found that it produced a substance capable of killing many dangerous bacteria, including those that caused pneumonia, meningitis and infected wounds. He named the substance penicillin, after the mould, which was called Penicillium.

At the time, infections that we now consider minor were often fatal. A small cut from a rose thorn could become infected and kill a healthy person. Women frequently died from infections after childbirth. Soldiers died not just from their wounds, but from the infections that followed. Doctors had no reliable way to stop bacteria once they entered the body.

However, Fleming faced a problem. Penicillin was very difficult to produce in large quantities and was unstable, breaking down quickly. He published his findings, but for more than ten years, few people paid attention.

Then, in 1939, as the Second World War began, two scientists at Oxford University, Howard Florey and Ernst Chain, read Fleming's paper. They led a team that discovered how to purify and concentrate penicillin. In 1941, they tested it on a police officer dying from an infection. He began to recover dramatically, but they ran out of penicillin before he was cured, and he died. It was a tragic demonstration of both its power and its scarcity.

The team, with help from American companies, developed methods to mass-produce the drug. By the end of the war, penicillin was saving thousands of lives.

In 1945, Fleming, Florey and Chain shared the Nobel Prize in Medicine. Penicillin became the first widely used antibiotic and has since saved hundreds of millions of lives.

Fleming later said, "One sometimes finds what one is not looking for." His discovery reminds us that curiosity, and the willingness to notice the unexpected, can change the world.`, "contaminated, mould, penicillin, pneumonia, fatal, unstable, purify, antibiotic",
"What did Fleming notice in the dish?|A clear ring around mould where bacteria had been destroyed;Why did it take years before penicillin saved many lives?|It was hard to produce in large amounts and broke down quickly, until Florey and Chain found how to purify it;What does Fleming's quote suggest about discovery?|Important discoveries can come from noticing unexpected things");

S(17, "The Neighbour's Wall", `The wall between the Ahmed family's house and the Qureshi family's house had been crumbling for years. It was made of old mud bricks, and every monsoon, a few more fell away. Nobody fixed it, because nobody could agree who owned it.

Mr. Ahmed insisted the wall belonged to the Qureshis. Mr. Qureshi insisted it belonged to the Ahmeds. The two men had argued about it for so long that they no longer spoke at all. Their wives communicated through their children. Their children were forbidden from playing together.

Hiba Ahmed and Sana Qureshi were eleven. They had been best friends until the argument began, when they were seven. Now they passed each other in the lane without speaking, though sometimes, when no adults were watching, they exchanged small smiles.

One night in July, during a violent storm, the wall finally collapsed. In the morning, both families came out to find a pile of mud and broken bricks between their courtyards. For the first time in four years, nothing separated them.

The two fathers stood on either side of the rubble, glaring at each other.

"Your wall," said Mr. Ahmed coldly, "has damaged my mother's vegetable garden."

"My wall?" said Mr. Qureshi. "Your wall has crushed my goat shed."

Hiba looked across the rubble at Sana. Sana looked back. Without saying a word, both girls stepped forward, picked up a brick each, and began stacking them neatly at the edge of the pile.

Their fathers stared at them.

"What are you doing?" Mr. Qureshi demanded.

"Clearing it," said Sana. "Someone has to."

Hiba's little brother joined them. Then Sana's sister. Then, after a long, uncomfortable pause, Mrs. Ahmed rolled up her sleeves and picked up a brick too. Within minutes, Mrs. Qureshi was beside her.

The two men stood watching their families work together. Finally, Mr. Ahmed cleared his throat. "Perhaps," he said stiffly, "we could share the cost of a new wall."

Mr. Qureshi considered this. "Perhaps," he said, "it could be a lower wall. With a gate."

They rebuilt it together over three weekends. The new wall reached only to the girls' shoulders, and it had a small wooden gate in the middle. Nobody ever discussed who owned it. The gate was rarely closed.`, "crumbling, monsoon, communicated, forbidden, collapsed, rubble, glaring, considered",
"Why hadn't anyone fixed the wall?|The two fathers couldn't agree who owned it;How did the girls change the situation?|They started clearing the rubble together, and the families joined in;What does a low wall with a gate suggest about the families now?|They are friendly and open to each other again");

S(17, "The Unsent Letter", `Zubair found the letter by accident. He was searching his grandfather's old trunk for a cricket bat when he noticed an envelope tucked inside the lining, yellowed with age. It was addressed, in his grandfather's careful handwriting, to a man named Harish Chandra in Amritsar.

It had never been posted.

Zubair knew better than to read other people's letters. But curiosity won. He unfolded the thin paper and began to read.

"My dear Harish," it began. "It has been three years since we parted at the railway station in Lahore. I still remember your face at the window of the train. I have tried to write to you a hundred times, but I never know what to say."

The letter described a childhood friendship: two boys who had grown up in the same lane, played cricket together, shared sweets during Diwali and Eid. Then, in 1947, when the country was divided at Partition, Harish's family had fled to India, and the two friends were separated forever.

"I want you to know," the letter continued, "that I kept your bat. I play with it every Sunday. Some days I hit it so hard I imagine the ball might fly all the way across the border to you."

Zubair looked down at the trunk. Beneath where the letter had been, wrapped in an old shawl, was a cricket bat, worn smooth at the handle.

He took the letter to his grandfather, who was sitting in the courtyard. When the old man saw it, his face changed. For a long time, he said nothing.

"Why didn't you send it?" Zubair asked gently.

His grandfather looked at the letter. "At first, it was too painful. Then, the longer I waited, the more foolish it seemed. Seventy years. He has probably forgotten me."

Zubair thought about this. Then he did something his grandfather did not expect. Over the next month, with help from his teacher and a website that reconnected families separated by Partition, he searched for Harish Chandra's family.

He found a granddaughter in Delhi. Harish had died five years earlier. But she wrote back with a photograph. In it, an elderly man sat in a garden, holding a faded photograph of two boys with a cricket bat.

On the back, in Hindi and in shaky English, was written: "My friend Yousaf. I never forgot."

Zubair's grandfather held the photograph for a very long time.`, "lining, Amritsar, curiosity, Partition, separated, foolish, reconnected, granddaughter",
"Who was Harish Chandra?|Grandfather's childhood friend who moved to India at Partition in 1947;Why didn't Grandfather send the letter?|At first it was too painful, then he thought it was too late and his friend had forgotten him;What does the photograph show about Harish?|He never forgot his friend either");

S(17, "The Price of a Shortcut", `Every morning, Faisal took the long road to school. It curved around the edge of the old brick kiln, past the canal, and through the bazaar. It took forty minutes.

There was a shortcut. It cut directly across the abandoned kiln yard, where piles of broken bricks, rusted machinery and deep pits were scattered everywhere. It took only fifteen minutes. A large sign at the entrance said "DANGER. KEEP OUT." Faisal's mother had made him promise never to go that way.

His friend Waleed laughed at him. "Everyone uses the shortcut," he said. "The sign is only there because the owner doesn't want trouble. Nothing's going to happen."

Faisal was tempted. He was always late, and the teacher had warned him that one more late mark would mean detention. But he had made a promise.

One Monday, he overslept. He knew that if he took the long road, he would definitely be late. He stood at the entrance to the kiln yard, staring at the sign. His mother would never know.

He took one step through the gap in the fence. Then another. He told himself it was just this once.

Halfway across, he heard a shout. It came from behind a pile of rubble. He hurried toward it and found Waleed lying at the bottom of a shallow pit, clutching his ankle, his face white with pain. The edge of the pit had crumbled beneath him.

Faisal's first instinct was to climb down. Then he saw how loose the edges were. If he fell too, nobody would know where they were.

Instead, he ran back to the road and stopped the first adult he saw, a man selling vegetables from a cart. The man fetched a ladder and two other men. Together, they lifted Waleed out carefully. His ankle was broken.

At the hospital, Waleed's mother thanked Faisal over and over. "If you hadn't been there," she said, "he might have lain there all day."

Faisal felt sick with confusion. He had broken his promise. If he had kept it, he wouldn't have been there to help. Was that a good thing or a bad thing?

That evening, he told his mother everything. She listened silently. Then she said, "You did the right thing by getting help instead of climbing down. But don't think the shortcut was right just because it turned out well. Waleed thought nothing would happen, too."

Faisal took the long road the next morning. He left twenty-five minutes earlier.`, "kiln, abandoned, scattered, tempted, detention, rubble, instinct, confusion",
"Why did Faisal take the shortcut that day?|He overslept and didn't want to be late again;Why didn't Faisal climb down into the pit?|The edges were loose, and if he fell too, nobody would know where they were;What did his mother mean that the shortcut wasn't right just because it turned out well?|A risky choice is still risky even if things end well, as Waleed's accident showed");

S(17, "The Silent Student", `Nobody in Class Seven had ever heard Areeba speak. She had joined the school at the beginning of the year, transferred from a village in Balochistan. She sat in the back corner, did her work neatly, and answered questions in writing when the teacher asked her directly. At break time, she ate alone under the neem tree.

The other girls had theories. "She's proud," said Huma. "She thinks she's better than us." "She doesn't know Urdu well enough," said someone else. "She's just strange," said a third.

Rida, who sat in front of her, wasn't sure what to think. Sometimes she noticed Areeba sketching in the margins of her notebook: tiny, intricate drawings of birds and mountains and camels. They were remarkably good.

One day, during an art lesson, the teacher asked each student to draw their home. While the others drew houses and streets, Areeba drew a mountain valley with a small mud house, a tree, and an old woman sitting in the doorway. Underneath, very small, she had written a single word: "Nani."

Rida glanced at the drawing and then at Areeba's face. Her eyes looked red.

After school, Rida found her under the neem tree. She sat down beside her without saying anything. For a long time, they just sat there.

Then, very quietly, Areeba spoke. Her voice was soft and slightly hoarse, as though she hadn't used it in a while. "My grandmother raised me," she said. "She died in the winter. That's why I came here, to live with my aunt."

Rida didn't know what to say. So she just said, "I'm sorry."

Areeba nodded. "In my village, everyone knew me. Here, nobody does. I didn't know how to start talking, so I just didn't."

Rida thought about the theories the other girls had made up. None of them had been right. None of them had even asked.

The next day, Rida sat with Areeba under the neem tree at break. She brought her own sketchbook. The day after that, Huma came too, a little awkwardly, and asked Areeba to show her how she drew such delicate birds.

By the end of term, Areeba was still quiet. But she spoke when she wanted to, and when she laughed, which happened more often now, the sound surprised everyone, including herself.`, "transferred, theories, intricate, remarkably, hoarse, awkwardly, delicate, surprised",
"Why had Areeba moved to the new school?|Her grandmother, who raised her, died, so she came to live with her aunt;What does the word Nani under her drawing tell us?|She was drawing her grandmother and missed her;What mistake had the other girls made?|They made up reasons about Areeba without ever asking her");

S(17, "The Kite Runner's Choice", `During the spring festival, the sky above the old city filled with hundreds of kites. Boys and girls battled from the rooftops, trying to cut each other's strings with glass-coated thread. When a kite was cut, it drifted free, and dozens of children raced through the narrow lanes to catch it. Whoever caught it could keep it.

Ahsan was the fastest kite runner in his neighbourhood. He knew every alley, every shortcut across rooftops, every wall that could be climbed. His room was full of captured kites.

This year, the biggest prize was a magnificent kite owned by a wealthy boy named Taimur. It was enormous, made of shimmering silk in red and gold, with a long tail of ribbons. Everyone wanted it.

In the afternoon, a fierce battle broke out. Taimur's kite was finally cut. As it drifted over the rooftops, a crowd of runners chased after it, Ahsan leading the pack.

The kite floated down toward a narrow lane. Ahsan sprinted around the corner and saw it: caught on a low branch, just within reach. He was far ahead of the others. In a moment, it would be his.

But as he reached for it, he saw a small girl standing beneath the tree. She was perhaps six, wearing a faded dress, and she was staring up at the kite with longing. She had clearly been there first, but she was too small to reach.

Ahsan hesitated. Behind him, he could hear the other runners approaching. He had wanted this kite all year.

He reached up and unhooked it from the branch. Then he knelt down and placed it in the girl's arms.

Her eyes widened. She clutched the kite against her chest as though afraid someone would take it away.

The other runners arrived, panting. "Who got it?" one demanded.

"She did," said Ahsan. "She was here first."

Some of the boys grumbled, but none of them argued. They wandered off, looking for other kites.

Later that evening, Taimur himself appeared at Ahsan's door. He had heard what happened. Ahsan expected him to be angry, but instead, Taimur held out a spool of his special glass thread. "I don't need the kite back," he said. "But I wanted to meet the boy who gave away the best kite in the city."

The next year, they flew kites from the same rooftop.`, "festival, magnificent, wealthy, shimmering, sprinted, longing, hesitated, spool",
"Why did Ahsan give the kite to the girl?|She had been there first and was too small to reach it;What does the girl clutching the kite tightly suggest?|She had probably never had a kite like it and was afraid someone would take it;Why did Taimur come to Ahsan's house?|He was impressed by Ahsan's kindness and wanted to meet him");

S(17, "The Tea Seller's Son", `Shoaib's father sold tea from a wooden cart outside the bus station. Every day, from five in the morning until late at night, he boiled milk and tea leaves in a dented aluminium pot and poured it into small glasses for travellers, drivers and porters.

After school, Shoaib helped. He washed glasses, collected coins, and carried cups to the drivers who couldn't leave their buses. He was quick and polite, and the regular customers liked him.

But Shoaib was ashamed. His classmates' fathers were shopkeepers, clerks and teachers. When anyone at school asked what his father did, he mumbled vaguely and changed the subject.

One day, the school announced a parents' evening. Each parent would be invited to speak briefly about their work. Shoaib felt his stomach drop. He imagined his father in his stained shirt, standing in front of the whole class talking about tea.

He didn't tell his father about it. He threw the invitation in a bin on the way home.

But a week later, on the evening of the event, Shoaib was surprised to see his father walking through the school gate. He was wearing his only good shirt and carrying a large flask. His teacher had sent a second invitation by hand.

Shoaib wanted to disappear.

When it was his father's turn to speak, he stood in front of the class and smiled nervously. "I sell tea," he said simply. "At the bus station."

Someone giggled. Shoaib stared at his desk.

"Every day," his father continued, "I meet people travelling from every corner of the country. Drivers who haven't slept in two days. Students going to university for the first time. Old men going to visit grandchildren they've never met. Soldiers going home on leave. Some of them have no money, so I give them tea anyway. A hot drink can make a long journey feel shorter."

The classroom had gone quiet.

"I didn't finish school," his father said. "I want my son to have choices I never had." He opened the flask. "But today, I've brought tea for everyone."

He poured small cups and handed them around. The teacher tasted hers and closed her eyes. "This is the best tea I've ever had," she said.

That night, walking home beside his father, Shoaib felt something he hadn't expected: pride. The next day, when a classmate asked what his father did, he answered immediately.`, "aluminium, porters, regular, ashamed, vaguely, invitation, flask, immediately",
"Why did Shoaib throw away the invitation?|He was ashamed of his father's job selling tea;What did the father's speech show about his work?|That he meets all kinds of travellers and helps them with kindness;How had Shoaib's feelings changed by the end?|He felt proud of his father and answered honestly");

S(17, "The Great Wall of Plastic", `Every year, around eleven million tonnes of plastic enter the world's oceans. That is roughly equal to one rubbish truck full of plastic being emptied into the sea every single minute. If nothing changes, scientists warn that by 2050 there could be more plastic in the ocean, by weight, than fish.

Plastic is an extraordinary material. It is cheap, light, waterproof and durable. These qualities have made it essential for everything from medical equipment to food packaging. But the same durability that makes plastic useful also makes it a serious environmental problem. Most plastics take hundreds of years to break down. Instead of disappearing, they gradually split into smaller and smaller pieces called microplastics, tiny fragments smaller than a grain of rice.

Plastic reaches the ocean in many ways. Rubbish dropped on streets is washed into drains, then rivers, then the sea. Poorly managed rubbish dumps near rivers or coasts allow plastic to blow or wash away. Fishing nets lost at sea, sometimes called "ghost nets," continue to trap animals for years.

Pakistan's rivers, including the Indus, carry large amounts of plastic waste toward the Arabian Sea. Along the beaches of Karachi, plastic bottles, bags and wrappers often wash up after the monsoon.

The effects on marine life are severe. Sea turtles mistake floating plastic bags for jellyfish, their natural food, and swallow them. Seabirds feed bottle caps to their chicks. Whales have been found dead with stomachs full of plastic. Fish eat microplastics, and when people eat fish, those particles may enter our bodies too. Scientists are still studying what effect this has on human health.

Solving the problem requires action at many levels. Governments can ban single-use plastics, such as thin shopping bags and straws. In 2019, Pakistan banned plastic bags in Islamabad, and other cities followed. Companies can design packaging that is easier to recycle or reuse. Cities can improve rubbish collection and recycling systems.

Individuals can make a difference too. Carrying a cloth bag, refusing plastic straws, using a refillable water bottle, and properly disposing of rubbish all help. Some communities in Karachi organise regular beach clean-ups, where volunteers collect thousands of kilograms of plastic in a single day.

The problem is enormous. But so is the number of people who can help solve it. Every piece of plastic kept out of the ocean is one less piece that a turtle or a fish might swallow.`, "tonnes, durable, environmental, microplastics, fragments, marine, single-use, disposing",
"Why is plastic's durability a problem?|It takes hundreds of years to break down and turns into microplastics instead of disappearing;How can plastic in the ocean end up affecting people?|Fish eat microplastics, and people eat fish;What is one thing individuals can do to help?|Carry a cloth bag, refuse plastic straws, use a refillable bottle, or dispose of rubbish properly");

S(17, "The Wedding Dress", `Two days before her sister's wedding, Nimra did something terrible.

She had been told not to touch the wedding dress. It hung in the corner of the room, wrapped in a cotton sheet: a deep red silk lehenga, heavy with gold embroidery, that her mother had saved for years to buy. Her sister Saima had cried with happiness when she first tried it on.

But Nimra was fourteen and curious, and when everyone went to the bazaar, she unwrapped it to look more closely. She held it up against herself in front of the mirror, admiring the way the gold thread caught the light.

Then she noticed she was holding a cup of chai in her other hand. She hadn't even realised. As she turned, the cup tipped, and a stream of brown tea splashed across the front of the skirt.

Nimra froze. Then she grabbed a cloth and scrubbed frantically, which only spread the stain wider. Within minutes, a large brown patch had spread across the red silk.

She wrapped the dress in the sheet again and hung it back in the corner. Her hands were shaking. When her family came home, she said nothing.

That night, she couldn't sleep. She imagined Saima's face when she discovered the stain on her wedding day, in front of all the guests. She imagined her mother, who had sacrificed so much. Each hour, the secret grew heavier.

Before dawn, she woke her mother. Through tears, she explained everything.

Her mother was silent for a long moment. Then she got up, lit a lamp, and examined the dress. Her face was grim. "Silk is difficult," she said finally. "Water can make it worse."

Nimra burst into tears again.

"But," her mother continued, "your Khala Rehana was a tailor for thirty years. If anyone can help, she can."

They took the dress to her aunt's house at sunrise. Khala Rehana inspected it, clicked her tongue, and set to work with special powders, steam and patience. It took the entire day. The stain didn't disappear completely, but it faded to a faint shadow. Then, with remarkable skill, she stitched a delicate border of gold embroidery over the area, so cleverly that it looked like part of the original design.

On the wedding day, Saima looked radiant. Nobody noticed anything. Later that night, Nimra confessed to her sister.

Saima laughed and hugged her. "Then my dress has a story," she said. "That makes it even more special."`, "lehenga, embroidery, admiring, frantically, sacrificed, Khala, inspected, radiant",
"What happened to the wedding dress?|Nimra spilled tea on it while admiring it;Why did Nimra tell her mother before dawn?|The secret felt too heavy and she imagined how hurt everyone would be;How did Saima react when Nimra confessed?|She laughed, hugged her, and said the dress now had a story");

S(17, "The Boy Who Couldn't Read", `Hassan was thirteen, and he had a secret: he couldn't really read.

He could recognise some words. He could sound out short ones slowly. But when he looked at a page of text, the letters seemed to swim and swap places. The words wouldn't stay still.

He had become an expert at hiding it. He memorised the stories teachers read aloud. He copied answers from his neighbour. When asked to read in class, he would cough, ask to use the toilet, or crack a joke so that everyone laughed and the teacher moved on. His teachers thought he was lazy and disruptive. His father said he would never amount to anything.

Hassan began to believe them.

Then, in his final year at middle school, a new teacher arrived: Miss Farzana. She noticed things. She noticed that Hassan's spoken answers were often clever and thoughtful, but his written work was a mess. She noticed that he held books strangely, sometimes tilting them, and that his eyes moved oddly across the page.

One day, she asked him to stay after class. Hassan prepared himself for another lecture. Instead, she gave him a short passage and asked him to read it aloud, just to her.

He struggled through it, his face burning. When he finished, he waited for her to sigh or frown.

Instead, she asked, "Do the letters move?"

Hassan stared at her. Nobody had ever asked him that before. Slowly, he nodded.

Miss Farzana explained that she thought he might have dyslexia, a difference in the way the brain processes written language. It had nothing to do with intelligence. Many brilliant people had it: scientists, artists, inventors, writers. It simply meant he needed different ways to learn.

For the first time in years, Hassan felt something like hope.

She tried different methods with him. She printed text on yellow paper instead of white, which made the letters steadier. She taught him to use a ruler under each line. She let him listen to recordings of books while following the words. She broke long words into smaller chunks.

It wasn't easy, and progress was slow. Some days, he wanted to give up. But gradually, the words began to stay still.

At the end of the year, Hassan stood in front of the class and read an entire poem aloud. He stumbled twice. But he finished. When he looked up, Miss Farzana was smiling, and so, to his astonishment, was his father, standing at the back of the room.`, "recognise, disruptive, amount, lecture, dyslexia, intelligence, recordings, astonishment",
"How did Hassan hide that he couldn't read well?|He memorised stories, copied answers, and made jokes or excuses to avoid reading aloud;What did Miss Farzana notice that others had missed?|His spoken answers were clever, but his reading and writing didn't match, and he tilted books;What is dyslexia, according to the story?|A difference in how the brain processes written language, not a lack of intelligence");

S(17, "Malala's Voice", `On 9 October 2012, a fifteen-year-old girl named Malala Yousafzai was travelling home from school in a crowded van in the Swat Valley when a gunman stopped the vehicle. He asked, "Who is Malala?" and then shot her in the head.

The attack shocked the world. But to understand why anyone would target a schoolgirl, we need to go back a few years.

Malala was born in 1997 in Mingora, the largest town in the Swat Valley, a region of green mountains and rivers in northern Pakistan. Her father, Ziauddin, ran a school for girls and boys and believed passionately in education for everyone.

In 2007, an extremist group took control of much of the Swat Valley. They imposed harsh rules, destroyed music shops, and banned girls from attending school. More than a hundred girls' schools were blown up.

Malala, then only eleven, refused to stay silent. Using a false name, she began writing a blog for the BBC's Urdu service, describing daily life under the extremists and her fear that she might never be allowed to study again. Later, she began speaking publicly, giving interviews on television about every girl's right to education.

Her courage made her well known, and it also made her a target.

After being shot, Malala was flown to Birmingham in the United Kingdom for specialist treatment. Doctors weren't sure she would survive. But after months of surgery and rehabilitation, she recovered.

Instead of being silenced, she became even louder. On her sixteenth birthday, she spoke at the United Nations in New York. "One child, one teacher, one book and one pen can change the world," she said.

In 2014, at seventeen, Malala became the youngest person ever to receive the Nobel Peace Prize. She shared it with Kailash Satyarthi, an Indian campaigner against child labour. She used her prize money to help build schools and founded the Malala Fund, which works to ensure that girls everywhere can receive twelve years of free, safe education.

She later studied at Oxford University and graduated in 2020. Today, she continues to campaign for education around the world.

Malala's story is often told as one of extraordinary bravery. But she has said that she is just one of millions of girls who want to learn. Today, about 120 million girls around the world are still out of school. Her message is that education is not a privilege for a few, but a right for every child.`, "Yousafzai, passionately, extremist, imposed, blog, rehabilitation, campaigner, privilege",
"Why did the extremists target Malala?|She spoke out publicly for girls' right to education when they had banned it;What did Malala do after she recovered?|She kept campaigning, spoke at the UN, won the Nobel Peace Prize, and founded the Malala Fund;What does privilege mean in the last sentence?|Something only some people get, rather than a right for everyone");

S(17, "The Village Doctor", `When Dr. Ayesha graduated from medical school in Lahore, she was offered a well-paid position at a modern hospital in the city. Her parents were thrilled. Her classmates envied her.

She turned it down.

Instead, she took a job at a small government clinic in a remote village in southern Punjab, where she had spent her childhood summers with her grandparents. The clinic had two rooms, a leaking roof, and no doctor. The last one had left three years earlier.

Her parents were horrified. "We didn't spend all that money on your education so you could waste it in a village," her father said.

Dr. Ayesha didn't argue. She simply packed her bags.

The first months were exhausting. Patients arrived from dozens of surrounding villages, often walking for hours. Many had illnesses that had gone untreated for years because there was nobody to see them. There was no reliable electricity, so she stored vaccines in a cool box packed with ice brought from town each morning. She worked fourteen hours a day.

Some villagers were suspicious. A young woman doctor was unusual, and some men refused to let their wives and daughters be treated by her, or by anyone. Others preferred traditional healers.

Slowly, things changed. She treated a child with severe pneumonia who would otherwise have died. She delivered twins safely during a night when the electricity failed, by the light of mobile phone torches. She organised vaccination days and trained two local women as health workers who could visit homes.

Word spread. The men who had been suspicious began bringing their families. The traditional healer, an elderly woman named Mai Jindan, began sending her patients to the clinic when their illnesses were serious. In return, Dr. Ayesha learned from Mai Jindan which local plants the villagers used and which might be harmful.

Two years later, her father visited for the first time. He arrived expecting to find her miserable. Instead, he found a long line of patients outside the clinic, a newly repaired roof paid for by the villagers themselves, and his daughter laughing with an old woman over a pot of herbal tea.

He sat on a bench and watched her work all afternoon. When the last patient left, he said nothing for a long time. Then he said, "Your grandmother would have been proud." Dr. Ayesha understood that this was his way of saying that he was proud too.`, "graduated, envied, remote, horrified, exhausting, vaccines, suspicious, pneumonia",
"Why did Dr. Ayesha go to the village instead of the city hospital?|The village had no doctor, and she had spent childhood summers there with her grandparents;How did Dr. Ayesha win the villagers' trust?|By saving lives, training local health workers, and working with the traditional healer;What did her father mean by saying her grandmother would have been proud?|It was his way of saying he was proud of her too");

S(17, "The Football", `In the refugee camp on the outskirts of Peshawar, there was only one football. It belonged to a boy named Jawad, whose uncle had brought it from Kabul. It was old and patched, but it was round, and it bounced, and every child in the camp wanted to play with it.

Jawad was the most popular boy in the camp. Every afternoon, dozens of children gathered around his tent, hoping to be chosen for his team. Jawad decided who played and who didn't. He chose his friends first, then the best players. The smallest and slowest children rarely got a turn.

One of them was a thin, quiet boy called Noor. He had arrived only a month earlier with his mother. Nobody knew much about him, except that his father had not come with them.

Every day, Noor stood at the edge of the dusty field, watching. Jawad never chose him.

One afternoon, Jawad kicked the ball too hard. It sailed over the barbed wire fence at the edge of the camp and landed in a ditch on the other side. Children were forbidden from leaving the camp without permission. The guard at the gate would never let them out to fetch a football.

The children stared at the fence in dismay. Jawad looked as though he might cry.

Noor walked quietly to a section of the fence where the wire sagged near the ground. He lay flat, wriggled underneath with practised ease, retrieved the ball from the ditch, and wriggled back. He handed it to Jawad without a word.

Everyone was astonished. "How did you know how to do that?" someone asked.

Noor shrugged. "On the journey here," he said, "we had to cross many fences."

The field went silent. The children looked at Noor differently. They understood, without anyone explaining, that his journey had been much harder than theirs.

Jawad turned the football over in his hands. Then he held it out to Noor. "You pick the teams today," he said.

Noor looked surprised. Then he chose. He picked the smallest children first, the ones who never played, and then the others. Jawad was the last one he picked.

Nobody complained. That afternoon, everyone played. And from then on, Jawad made sure that whoever picked the teams chose someone new first.`, "refugee, outskirts, patched, barbed, forbidden, dismay, retrieved, astonished",
"Why did Noor never get to play at first?|Jawad chose his friends and the best players, and Noor was small and new;What does Noor's comment about fences suggest about his journey?|It was difficult and maybe dangerous, as a refugee crossing borders;Why do you think Noor picked the smallest children first?|He knew how it felt to be left out");

S(17, "The Secret Garden of the Old Fort", `In the heart of Lahore stands the Lahore Fort, a vast complex of palaces, halls and gardens built mainly by the Mughal emperors in the sixteenth and seventeenth centuries. For centuries, it was the seat of royal power. Today, it is a UNESCO World Heritage Site, visited by thousands of people every year.

The fort covers more than twenty hectares. Its massive walls, made of red sandstone and brick, once protected the emperors and their courts. Inside, the buildings reflect the extraordinary wealth and artistic skill of the Mughal era.

Perhaps the most dazzling building is the Sheesh Mahal, or Palace of Mirrors, built in 1631 by Emperor Shah Jahan, who also built the Taj Mahal. Its walls and ceilings are covered with thousands of tiny pieces of convex mirror set into white marble and plaster in intricate patterns. According to legend, a single candle lit inside the hall would be reflected so many times that the entire room would sparkle like a sky full of stars.

Another remarkable feature is the Picture Wall, one of the largest murals in the world. It stretches nearly fifteen hundred feet along the outer walls of the fort and is decorated with glazed tiles and mosaics. They depict elephants, camels, horses, hunting scenes, angels, and even battles between mythical creatures. Restorers have spent years carefully cleaning and repairing these tiles, many of which had been damaged by weather and neglect.

The Mughals were passionate about gardens. They believed a garden should be a reflection of paradise, and they designed them according to strict geometric rules. The traditional Mughal garden, called a charbagh, is divided into four equal parts by water channels, representing the four rivers of paradise described in religious texts. Fountains, fruit trees and flowering plants filled these spaces, providing cool shade in the fierce summer heat.

The fort was later used by Sikh rulers in the nineteenth century, and then by the British, who used parts of it as military barracks. Each ruler left their own marks, adding or changing buildings.

Walking through the fort today, visitors can see how architecture tells the story of a city. Every arch, every tile, and every faded painting holds a memory of the people who built it and lived within its walls. Preserving such places helps us understand where we come from.`, "Mughal, complex, hectares, convex, intricate, murals, charbagh, barracks",
"Who built the Sheesh Mahal?|Emperor Shah Jahan, in 1631;What do the four parts of a charbagh garden represent?|The four rivers of paradise;What does the last paragraph suggest about why old buildings matter?|They hold the history of a city and help us understand where we come from");

S(17, "The Debt", `When Yasir's father fell ill and couldn't work for three months, the family borrowed money from Haji Sahib, the wealthiest man in the village. He lent it without hesitation and said they could pay it back whenever they were able.

Over the next two years, Yasir's father slowly repaid the debt, a little each month. He was proud and hated owing anyone anything. Finally, there was only one payment left.

On the day it was due, Yasir's father counted the notes carefully and sent Yasir to deliver them.

Haji Sahib's house was large, with a courtyard full of pomegranate trees. A servant showed Yasir in. Haji Sahib was sitting on a carpet, looking at some papers. He looked older than Yasir remembered and very tired.

Yasir handed him the envelope. "From my father," he said. "The last payment."

Haji Sahib opened it and counted the money. Then, unexpectedly, he smiled. "Your father is an honest man," he said. "Do you know how many people in this village have repaid what they borrowed from me?"

Yasir shook his head.

"Very few," said Haji Sahib. He gestured to the papers in front of him. They were lists of names and numbers. "Most people forget, or pretend to forget. Some avoid me in the street."

Yasir didn't know what to say.

Then Haji Sahib did something strange. He tore up the list in front of him, slowly and deliberately, until it was nothing but small pieces of paper.

"Why did you do that?" Yasir asked.

The old man looked at him. "I am getting old," he said. "I've realised that if I spend my last years angry about money people owe me, I'll die an unhappy man. These people needed help. I gave it. Whether they return it is their choice, and their conscience." He paused. "But your father's honesty has reminded me why I helped in the first place."

He handed the envelope back to Yasir. "Tell your father the debt is paid. And tell him to use this money to send you to the secondary school in town."

Yasir walked home in a daze. When he told his father, the older man was silent for a long time. Finally, he said, "We will accept. But one day, Yasir, when you are able, you will help someone else in the same way. That is how this kind of debt is repaid."`, "hesitation, repaid, wealthiest, gestured, deliberately, conscience, daze, accept",
"Why did Haji Sahib tear up the list?|He didn't want to spend his last years angry about money people owed him;Why did Haji Sahib give the money back?|Yasir's father's honesty reminded him why he helped people, and he wanted Yasir to go to school;What did Yasir's father mean by how this kind of debt is repaid?|By helping someone else in the same way one day");

S(17, "The Storm Petrel", `On the coast of Sindh, in a fishing village built on stilts above the mangrove swamps, lived an old fisherman named Baba Juma. He was famous for one thing: he could read the weather better than anyone alive.

The younger fishermen had smartphones with weather apps. They laughed at Baba Juma's habit of studying the clouds, the colour of the sea, and the behaviour of the birds. "The app is more accurate, Baba," they said kindly. "It uses satellites."

Baba Juma just nodded and kept watching.

His grandson, Karim, was twelve and spent every spare moment with him. Baba Juma taught him to notice things. "When the swallows fly low over the water," he said, "rain is coming, because the insects they eat are pushed down by the heavy air. When the sea turns the colour of milky tea and the waves come in long and slow from the south, a storm is far out at sea, pushing them ahead of it."

One morning in June, the sky was clear and blue. The weather app predicted calm seas for three days. The fishermen prepared their boats for a long trip to the deep fishing grounds.

But Baba Juma stood on the jetty, frowning. The air felt heavy and strangely still. The waves were rolling in long and slow from the south-west. And Karim noticed something unusual: dozens of small, dark seabirds called storm petrels were flying toward the shore, skimming low over the water.

"Petrels only come in like this before a big storm," Baba Juma said quietly. "Tell your father not to go."

Karim ran to his father, who was loading nets into his boat. His father glanced at the clear sky and his phone. "The app says it's fine," he said, irritated.

Karim hesitated. Then he said, "Baba has been fishing for sixty years. The app has been working for three."

His father paused. He looked out at the sea, at the strange slow waves, at the petrels. Then he unloaded his nets. Three other fishermen, seeing him, decided to stay as well. Two crews went out anyway.

That night, a cyclone that the forecasts had predicted would stay out at sea changed course and struck the coast. The wind howled until dawn. The two crews that had gone out were rescued, battered but alive, after clinging to their overturned boats for hours.

The next week, the younger fishermen still checked their apps. But they also began coming to the jetty each morning to stand beside Baba Juma and watch the sky.`, "stilts, mangrove, behaviour, satellites, jetty, petrels, cyclone, battered",
"What signs told Baba Juma a storm was coming?|Heavy still air, long slow waves from the south-west, and storm petrels flying toward shore;Why did Karim's father change his mind?|Karim pointed out that Baba had sixty years of experience compared to the app's three;What lesson does the ending suggest?|Old knowledge and modern technology are both valuable, and it's wise to use both");

S(17, "The Translator", `When Fatima's family moved from their village in Khyber Pakhtunkhwa to Karachi, her parents could speak only Pashto. Fatima, who was ten, had learned Urdu at her village school and quickly picked up more from the neighbours. Within months, she was fluent.

Soon, she became the family translator.

She translated at the doctor's when her mother was sick. She translated at the bank when her father opened an account. She translated with the landlord, the electricity company, and the teacher at her brothers' school. She read official letters aloud and filled in forms. Wherever her parents went, Fatima went too, to be their voice.

At first, she felt proud. Her parents depended on her. But gradually, the responsibility grew heavy. Sometimes she had to miss school to accompany them. Sometimes she had to translate things she didn't fully understand, like medical words or legal terms, and she worried constantly about making a mistake. Once, at a government office, an official was rude to her father, and she had to translate his insults. She saw her father's face tighten, and she felt ashamed, though she had done nothing wrong.

She never told anyone how she felt. She thought complaining would be ungrateful.

One day, her teacher noticed she had missed school three times in a month and asked her to stay behind. Fatima tried to explain lightly, but her voice cracked, and she ended up telling her everything.

Her teacher listened carefully. Then she said something Fatima had never considered. "You've been doing an adult's job for two years. That's an enormous amount to carry. It's all right to find it hard."

The teacher contacted a community centre that offered free Urdu classes for adults. When Fatima suggested it to her parents, her father was reluctant. "At my age? I'll look foolish."

But her mother agreed to try. Every Tuesday and Thursday evening, she went to the community centre with other women from the neighbourhood. It was slow and difficult, but within a year, she could handle shopping, conversations with neighbours, and simple appointments on her own.

Seeing her progress, Fatima's father eventually joined a class too.

Fatima still helped sometimes, with complicated letters or difficult phone calls. But now she went to school every day. And one evening, she came home to find her mother writing a shopping list in Urdu, slowly and carefully, without asking for help. Fatima felt a lightness she hadn't felt in two years.`, "Pashto, fluent, translator, responsibility, accompany, legal, ungrateful, reluctant",
"Why did Fatima become the family translator?|Her parents only spoke Pashto, and she had learned Urdu;Why didn't Fatima tell anyone how she felt?|She thought complaining would be ungrateful;Why did Fatima feel a lightness at the end?|Her mother could manage on her own, so the heavy responsibility was finally shared");
